# Negative control — undoes 61177e956e (`ProductGroup<NoInfer<Slug>>` ->
# `ProductGroup<Slug>`, same for `CustomArea`), reopening the
# array/variable-context authoring position: a `defineProductGroup`/
# `defineCustomArea` call sitting inside its own contextual type (a
# `PortalConfig`-typed `groups` array, a `ProductGroup`-typed variable) lets
# TypeScript infer `Slug = string` from that context instead of the passed
# literal, so a reserved slug like "billing" compiles clean there even
# though the bare, unannotated call still errors (design.md §D9 consequence
# 2, AC1.6, tasks.md 1.10c/1.11).
#
# Intended RED: an assertion that `defineProductGroup({ slug: "billing", ... })`
# fails `pnpm --filter @upmind-automation/portal-nuxt type-check` when
# written inline in a `PortalConfig`-typed `groups` array (or any other
# contextually-typed position), not just as a bare call. No such assertion
# exists yet: the committed fixture
# `app/portal/type-tests/reserved-pillar-segment.typecheck.ts` only exercises
# the bare-call shape — the one authoring position 61177e956e's fix did NOT
# need to touch, because it already errored before that fix too — so it
# stays green through this mutation. 61177e956e's own commit message states
# this ("it has no assertion at the array/variable-context authoring site
# this fix actually closes. That assertion is a prover addition"); filed for
# the prover to write it.
#
# Confirmed by isolating both shapes in one file, one run, before filing: the
# bare call still errors (TS2322 — `NotReservedSegment` itself is untouched);
# the same slug, same reserved segment, written inline in a `PortalConfig`'s
# `groups` array compiles clean. Exactly one error comes out of a file
# carrying both.

